From 464a32a9275bd819166a5024b64de166ed81f60a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Frank Reichenbach <frank.w.reichenbach@web.de>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 14:16:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 05/11] Resolve local refs inside a subschema with a urn: $id

Inside a subschema whose $id uses a scheme such as urn:, local refs
("#", "#/definitions/...") and plain-name $ids ("#foo") resolved
against the root schema. Validation then accepted invalid data,
rejected valid data, or failed to compile the schema.

The cause is urllib.parse.urljoin. It returns a relative reference
unchanged when the scheme of the base URI is not in
urllib.parse.uses_relative, for example urn:, tag: or s3:.

Add join_uri(). For a ref that starts with "#" and a base URI with
such a scheme, it keeps the base URI and replaces its fragment, as
RFC 3986 section 5.2.2 defines. Everything else still goes through
urljoin, so the result for http:, https:, file: and a schema without
$id doesn't change. All four urljoin calls in ref_resolver.py now use
join_uri().

Such refs now resolve as they do with an http: $id. That changes two
results that don't depend on the data:

- A schema whose ref inside such a subschema has its target only in
  the root schema compiled before and used that target. It now raises
  JsonSchemaDefinitionException.
- For a schema whose root $id uses such a scheme, compile_to_code()
  names the functions for "#/..." and "#foo" refs after the root $id.
  If the schema contains "$ref": "#", it no longer generates a second
  copy of the root function named validate.

Reverting the first call in resolving() alone changes no test result.
It uses join_uri() too, so that every URI built from a ref goes through
the same helper.

The tests are in tests/test_ref_urn.py. test_join_uri tests join_uri()
directly, for urn:, tag: and http: bases.
